\title{The systolic array fill bubble}
\taxon{Card}
\tag{card}
\meta{anki}{1786395976765}
\meta{anki-deck}{MLSys}

\prompt{Sketch the dataflow through a #{128 \times 128} systolic array ([[OGCRP]]). Where does the pipeline bubble come from?}{
  A 2D grid of 16,384 ALUs, each doing one multiply-add per cycle.

  \ul{
    \li{\strong{Weights} (RHS, #{128 \times 128}) are pushed \strong{down} from the top.}
    \li{\strong{Activations} (LHS, #{8 \times 128}) stream in from the \strong{left}.}
    \li{Each cell multiplies its overlapped pair, adds the partial sum arriving from above, and passes the result down.}
  }

  The \strong{bubble}: both operands are loaded \em{diagonally}, so the array needs a fill period before every cell is busy. Once filled, new LHS/RHS stream in back-to-back with no further bubble — which is why large matrices amortize the fill and small ones do not.
}
